# Logging in SpinDoctor

This rule covers the project-specific logger setup for `rms-spindoctor`. The generic
`PdsLogger` usage patterns (sectioning with `logger.open()`, `%`-style deferred
formatting, FCPath interaction, never using stdlib `logging`) live in
`logging` and apply here unchanged.

## 1. The Two Module-Level Loggers

`spindoctor.config.logger` defines two singleton loggers, configured through the
project config system and imported from `spindoctor.config`:

```python
from spindoctor.config import MAIN_LOGGER, IMAGE_LOGGER
```

| Logger | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `MAIN_LOGGER` | One per program run: top-level events, per-image progress, totals |
| `IMAGE_LOGGER` | One image inside one backend: models, techniques, reprojection, annotation |

`IMAGE_LOGGER` is a proxy, not a logger. Opening a section on it enters an
image scope; while that scope is open every nested section resolves to the same
real logger, so one image's records land in one file.

**A component declares which logger it belongs to.** `NavBase` subclasses carry
`log_role`, defaulting to `LogRole.IMAGE`. A component whose work spans a run
rather than an image sets `LogRole.MAIN` — `DataSet` does, because enumeration
belongs to no single image:

```python
class DataSet(ABC, NavBase):
    log_role: ClassVar[LogRole] = LogRole.MAIN
```

Logging through `IMAGE_LOGGER` with no image scope open is a defect, not a
mode. The record is routed to `MAIN_LOGGER` and the call site is reported once;
under `logging.strict_scope` it raises `LogScopeError` instead. That is off by
default; a test that drives a real pipeline turns it on with the
`strict_log_scope` fixture. It is deliberately not enabled suite-wide, because
a unit test exercising a model or technique in isolation calls it outside any
image scope by design. If a component trips this, it is bound to the wrong
logger — give it `LogRole.MAIN`.

**Programs that carry no logger.** The statistics report (`sd_stats_report`)
and the graphical programs (`sd_backplane_viewer`, `sd_mosaic_display`,
`sd_create_simulated_image`) write to the terminal with `print()` and take no
logging configuration. A report's output *is* terminal text for a person
reading it, and a GUI surfaces problems through dialogs. `sd_results_index` is
not one of them: it is infrastructure other programs depend on, so it carries a
main logger and a partial or failed pass appears in a run log. This is the one
documented exception to "never bare `print()` in library code", and it extends
to the `spindoctor.ui.mosaic_viewer` widgets those programs drive. Where a
converted call previously used `logger.exception`, follow the `print()` with
`traceback.print_exc()` so the stack trace is not silently lost.

## 2. Configuring a Run

A driver calls `add_logging_arguments(parser)` when building its parser and
`build_run_logging()` once in `main()`, after the configuration is loaded and
before anything is logged. That resolves the levels, installs them so any
component can read its own, builds the main logger, and stamps one timestamp
for the whole run:

```python
from spindoctor.cli.logging_args import add_logging_arguments
from spindoctor.config import build_run_logging

add_logging_arguments(cmdparser)                   # in the parser builder
...
run_logging = build_run_logging(PROGRAM_NAME, arguments, DEFAULT_CONFIG)
```

Pass `has_image_logger=False` for a program that does not process images
individually, so it rejects the image-logger flags rather than accepting and
ignoring them.

## 3. Per-Image Logs

Build the handlers for one image with `build_image_log_handlers()` and pass
them to the outermost `open()`, so they are active only for that image and are
removed when it is done. The caller closes them.

```python
from spindoctor.config import IMAGE_LOGGER, build_image_log_handlers

handlers, log_path = build_image_log_handlers(
    'nav', image_file.results_path_stub, run_logging.sinks, run_logging.levels,
    timestamp=run_logging.timestamp,
)
with IMAGE_LOGGER.open(str(image_url), handler=handlers):
    ...  # process the image
```

The backend name selects the log subtree, so an image's log for a given stage
is in one place whichever driver produced it. `log_path` is None when the file
sink is disabled; do not report a path that was never written.

Per the `filecache` rule the path is an `FCPath`, and you never `mkdir` the log
directory: the handler factory creates it, and `FCPath.mkdir` fails on a remote
root anyway.

## 3a. Opening a Component's Own Section

A component gets its own configurable level only by opening a section. A
`NavBase` subclass uses `self.log_section(title)`; a module-level function uses
the `logged_section(log_key, title)` decorator. Without one, a component's
records take the level of whatever section encloses them, and a configuration
key naming it would do nothing.

## 4. Quick Reference

| Task | Pattern |
|------|---------|
| Import loggers | `from spindoctor.config import MAIN_LOGGER, IMAGE_LOGGER` |
| Top-level events | `MAIN_LOGGER.info(...)` |
| Per-image events | `self._logger.info(...)` (follows the class's `log_role`) |
| Configure a run | `build_run_logging(PROGRAM_NAME, arguments, config)` once in `main()` |
| Per-image handlers | `build_image_log_handlers(backend, stub, sinks, levels)` passed to `open()` |
| A component's section | `self.log_section(title)`, or `@logged_section(key, title)` |
